question:
A 34-year-old woman carries the m.3243A>G heteroplasmic mutation with a 22% mutant load in her blood and is clinically asymptomatic. Over six years, she gives birth to three children who exhibit wildly divergent clinical presentations:
• Child 1 has a 12% mutant load and is completely healthy at age 10;
• Child 2 has a 58% mutant load and develops sensorineural hearing loss and insulin-dependent diabetes at age 8;
• Child 3 has an 86% mutant load and presents at age 2 with severe MELAS syndrome (intractable seizures, recurrent stroke-like episodes, and fatal lactic acidosis).
What embryological and developmental event during female gametogenesis accounts for this dramatic variation in mutant heteroplasmy among siblings born to the same mother?
